  geopolitics: "The transmission has changed shape this week and the change is worth being precise about, because this file got the attribution wrong twice. The energy complex lost 12% to 18% in five sessions - WTI 90.830 and minus 12.76%, Brent 98.320 and minus 13.32%, gasoline minus 12.03%, euro-area gas 85.59 and minus 17.66%, UK gas 212.25 and minus 17.60%, German gas minus 17.40%, heating oil minus 7.37% - and the largest single move was not the Qatar channel. Brent fell more than 8% on Wednesday alone, to a two-month low near 94.62, on the US midterm result, with Reuters tying the move to expectations that a Democratic sweep of both chambers curtails the administration's capacity for escalation and for tariffs. That matters for durability: a repricing of who can escalate is harder to reverse on a single headline than a premium unwound by a diplomatic rumour. The diplomatic leg is real but unresolved. One report has the US approving a Qatar-presented draft memorandum, with a phased sequence in which the US lifts the naval blockade and releases some frozen assets in exchange for the strait reopening and nuclear talks resuming; Iran's security council secretary called the US reply not convincing and tied any reopening to seven conditions including an end to the blockade, sanctions relief and the release of frozen funds. Nothing has been signed, and the widely cited 45-day ceasefire draft dates from April and is a different proposal from this one. Meanwhile the war itself has not paused, which is exactly why Friday's tape turned: energy rose on the day. Iran reported three more tanker attacks on Thursday, a Greek ship manager confirmed a seafarer was killed in a November 5 explosion on an oil products tanker in the Gulf believed to have been a missile strike, UKMTO data has attacks running near a record pace, flows remain under 60% of capacity, and one analyst quoted this week puts the probability of renewed military conflict at 80% with both benchmarks back above 100. So the premium is being priced out of a live conflict, and it has whipsawed three times in five sessions. The FX read is a ranking of who needs the premium. Six of these eight central banks named energy explicitly in their last statement - the ECB warning about indirect and second-round effects behind a 4.0% HICP that was 20.8% energy, Bailey making a further hike conditional on no progress toward resolving the conflict, the RBA listing fuel and commodity prices first among three reasons, the RBNZ flagging volatile oil and fuel behind a 4.4% December forecast, the Bank of Canada hiking expressly to stop high energy prices spreading, and the SNB forecasting a fourth-quarter rise that was 96% energy in October. All six are now defending cases built on a deflating input. The Fed named none of it, and because October's CPI lands Tuesday and November's not until December 10, it will vote on December 9 without ever seeing the disinflation in a print. That is the asymmetry under the dollar. The second-order read is the Canadian dollar, which is the most direct casualty - the Bank's stated rationale and its labour market both weakened in the same week - and the franc, which won the week outright on the European equity decline rather than on anything Swiss, with the SNB openly willing to intervene against it. Keep a USOIL long off the book while CAD is scored minus one: the two would contradict each other.",
